  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis place low over western Ontario, with\nridge over Alberta nudging into western Montana. Upper level\nanalysis places low over southern Saskatchewan with trough\ndropping south through the central Rockies. Over our area, a\nline of light snow affiliated with a passing jet streak\ncontinues to push easterly through south central North Dakota\ninto the James River Valley.\n\nFor today, cooler air continues to pull in behind the\naforementioned surface low, with highs pushing into the 30s over\nmost of our area, maybe a few 40s over our far southeast. As we\nget into tonight, compact upper low drops into north central\nNorth Dakota, and slides east into northeast North Dakota. Some\nlight snow may wrap around the system into northern parts of \nour area (around a 20 to 30 percent chance).\n\nOn Sunday, upper low continues its east-southeast track, with\nlow chances for snow lingering through the morning over north\ncentral North Dakota into the James River Valley. Low/trough\nthen push to the east while a surface high settles in later \nSunday into Monday. Quiet weather is expected, but temperatures\nwill be cool with highs in the mid 20s to mid 30s.\n\nThereafter, ensembles continue to show a progressive flow\npattern for the remainder of the week, with NBM keeping the\nforecast mainly dry. With that said, a variety of short waves\nmove through the area which could bring an occasional few flakes\nof snow. Temperatures will be on a gradual rebound, with highs\nin the 30s and 40s for Thanksgiving Day.",
